<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_bug_algorithms" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Bug Algorithms</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Bug algorithms are classical reactive navigation methods that combine direct goal-seeking with obstacle boundary following. They are simple, geometric, and robust enough to explain key tradeoffs in local navigation.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_bug1">
    <title>Bug 1</title>
    <p>In Bug 1, the robot moves directly toward the goal until it hits an obstacle. It then traces the full obstacle boundary to find the boundary point closest to the goal, returns to that point, and departs toward the goal from there.</p>
    <p>Bug 1 is systematic but can be inefficient because it may require full perimeter traversal before resuming progress.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_bug2">
    <title>Bug 2</title>
    <p>Bug 2 defines an m-line from start to goal. After obstacle contact, the robot follows the boundary until it intersects the m-line again at a point closer to the goal, then leaves the obstacle and continues along the m-line.</p>
    <p>Compared with Bug 1, Bug 2 often reduces unnecessary boundary travel while preserving simple reactive logic.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_bug_methods_notes">
    <title>Practical Notes</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Bug methods need only local sensing and a clear departure rule.</p></li>
      <li><p>They are useful for teaching obstacle handling before introducing full map-based planners.</p></li>
      <li><p>In complex spaces, they are often combined with higher-level planning or localization methods.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>
</section>
